= Solution
{parent=a}

Fix the <quantum enveloping algebra of sl2> convention
$$
KEK^{-1}=q^2E,\qquad KFK^{-1}=q^{-2}F,\qquad
EF-FE=\frac{K-K^{-1}}{q-q^{-1}},\qquad
[j]=\frac{q^j-q^{-j}}{q-q^{-1}}.
$$
The printed pair of matrices contains an error. On its first <basis> vector, the printed matrices give $(EF-FE)v_0=\epsilon[2]^2v_0$, whereas the defining commutator gives $\epsilon[2]v_0$. Since $q$ is not a <root of unity>, $[2]\ne0,1$: $[2]=0$ would give $q^2=-1$, and $[2]=1$ would give $q^2-q+1=0$. Thus **the two printed matrices cannot represent this algebra**. Keeping the printed $F$ matrix requires exchanging the two nonzero entries of the printed $E$ matrix. We derive that corrected pair rather than assume a classification theorem.

As $q$ is not a <root of unity>, successive applications of $E$ multiply $K$ <eigenvalues> by $q^2$. Finiteness of the <spectrum> therefore makes $E$ a <nilpotent operator>. Start from a $K$ <eigenvector> and apply $E$ until the last nonzero vector $v$; then $Ev=0$ and $Kv=\lambda v$, with $\lambda\ne0$. The defining commutator gives the <highest-weight string for quantum sl2>
$$
EF^jv=[j]\frac{\lambda q^{1-j}-\lambda^{-1}q^{j-1}}{q-q^{-1}}F^{j-1}v.
$$
For completeness, at $j=1$ this is the commutator applied to $v$. To advance from $j$ to $j+1$, write $EF^{j+1}v=F(EF^jv)+(K-K^{-1})F^jv/(q-q^{-1})$, use $KF^jv=\lambda q^{-2j}F^jv$, and combine the two coefficients; their sum is the displayed coefficient at $j+1$.

Let $N$ be the first index with $F^Nv=0$. Such an index exists because nonzero $F^jv$ have distinct $K$ <eigenvalues> $\lambda q^{-2j}$. The <linear span> of $v,Fv,\ldots,F^{N-1}v$ is stable under $K,E,F$, so simplicity makes it all of $V$. The distinct <eigenvalues> make these vectors independent; hence $N=3$. Applying the string formula to $F^3v=0$ gives
$$
0=[3]\frac{\lambda q^{-2}-\lambda^{-1}q^2}{q-q^{-1}}F^2v.
$$
As $[3]\ne0$ and $F^2v\ne0$, $\lambda^2=q^4$, so $\lambda=\epsilon q^2$ with $\epsilon=\pm1$.

Choose the <basis> $v_0=v$, $v_1=Fv/[2]$, $v_2=F^2v/[2]$. Then $Fv_0=[2]v_1$, $Fv_1=v_2$, $Ev_1=\epsilon v_0$ and $Ev_2=\epsilon[2]v_1$. The correct matrices in this <basis> are
$$
\boxed{E=\epsilon\begin{pmatrix}0&1&0\\0&0&[2]\\0&0&0\end{pmatrix},\quad
F=\begin{pmatrix}0&0&0\\{[2]}&0&0\\0&1&0\end{pmatrix},\quad
K=\epsilon\begin{pmatrix}q^2&0&0\\0&1&0\\0&0&q^{-2}\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
Alternatively, preserving the printed $E$ instead requires $F_{21}=1$, $F_{32}=[2]$. Both corrected pairs are related by a diagonal change of <basis>.

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Use the <comultiplication> $\Delta E=E\otimes K+1\otimes E$, $\Delta F=F\otimes1+K^{-1}\otimes F$, $\Delta K=K\otimes K$ of the <quantum enveloping algebra of sl2>. Choose a <basis> $u_0,u_1$ for $V_{1,1}$ with $Fu_0=u_1$, $Eu_1=u_0$, and $Ku_i=q^{1-2i}u_i$. Choose a <basis> $w_0,w_1,w_2$ for $V_{1,2}$ with
$$
Fw_0=w_1,\quad Fw_1=[2]w_2,\quad
Ew_1=[2]w_0,\quad Ew_2=w_1,\quad Kw_j=q^{2-2j}w_j,
$$
and all terminal raising or lowering actions zero. This is one of the corrected <bases> from part (a).

In the <tensor product of modules>, put
$$
\begin{aligned}
z_0&=u_0\otimes w_0,\\
z_1&=u_1\otimes w_0+q^{-1}u_0\otimes w_1,\\
z_2&=u_1\otimes w_1+q^{-2}u_0\otimes w_2,\\
z_3&=u_1\otimes w_2.
\end{aligned}
$$
The vector $z_0$ is killed by $E$ and has $K$ <eigenvalue> $q^3$. Direct application of the <comultiplication> gives
$$
Fz_0=z_1,\quad Fz_1=[2]z_2,\quad Fz_2=[3]z_3,\quad Fz_3=0,
$$
and the string formula gives $Ez_1=[3]z_0$, $Ez_2=[2]z_1$, $Ez_3=z_2$. Also $Kz_i=q^{3-2i}z_i$. Thus $W_3=\langle z_0,z_1,z_2,z_3\rangle$ is a <submodule> isomorphic to $V_{1,3}$.

The other highest vector and its lowered partner are
$$
\begin{aligned}
h_0&=[2]u_1\otimes w_0-q^2u_0\otimes w_1,\\
h_1=Fh_0&=u_1\otimes w_1-q[2]u_0\otimes w_2.
\end{aligned}
$$
Indeed $Eh_0=([2]q^2-q^2[2])u_0\otimes w_0=0$, $Kh_0=qh_0$, $Kh_1=q^{-1}h_1$, $Eh_1=h_0$ and $Fh_1=0$. Therefore $W_1=\langle h_0,h_1\rangle$ is a <submodule> isomorphic to $V_{1,1}$.

To verify that this is a direct sum, in the $q$ <weight space> the determinant of the coefficients of $z_1,h_0$ is $-q^2-q^{-1}[2]=-[3]\ne0$. In the $q^{-1}$ <weight space>, the coefficient determinant of $z_2,h_1$ is $-q[2]-q^{-2}=-[3]\ne0$. The extreme <weight spaces> are spanned by $z_0,z_3$. Hence these six vectors form a <basis> of the entire <tensor product of modules>. Each summand is a <simple module>: projecting a nonzero vector onto one of its distinct $K$ <weight spaces> and applying the nonzero raising and lowering coefficients recovers its entire <basis>. Consequently
$$
\boxed{V_{1,1}\otimes V_{1,2}=W_3\oplus W_1\cong V_{1,3}\oplus V_{1,1}.}
$$

